\section*{New Multi-Domain Physics Engine Achieves Machine-Precision Stability Under Extreme Space Weather Conditions}

\textbf{Lafayette, Louisiana — July 25, 2026} \\
Independent designer \textbf{C. Matthew Blackwell} announces the
\textbf{Astro-Geodetic Conservative Simulation Engine (AG-CSE v1.0)}, a
next-generation computational physics platform capable of real-time,
machine-precision modeling of Earth's magnetosphere, gravitational field,
and relativistic plasma dynamics under extreme astrophysical forcing.

AG-CSE integrates:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Relativistic fluid dynamics,
    \item High-order conservative PDE solvers,
    \item Adaptive Voronoi mesh refinement,
    \item Live ingestion of OMNIWeb solar wind vectors, IERS Earth orientation parameters, and EGM2008 gravitational harmonics.
\end{itemize}

\section*{Breakthrough in Multi-Physics Stability}
During full-scale stress testing across 2,048 NVIDIA H100 GPUs, AG-CSE demonstrated:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Total energy conservation error: $\Delta E / E_0 < 3.2 \times 10^{-13}$,
    \item Magnetic helicity invariant deviation: $< 10^{-11}$ over 50{,}000 steps,
    \item Zero negative-density events in relativistic mass-flux evolution,
    \item Global divergence control: $\nabla \cdot \mathbf{B} < 10^{-15}$,
    \item Stable magnetopause compression to $3.8 R_E$ under Carrington-class solar wind forcing.
\end{itemize}

\section*{High-Resolution Convergence}
AG-CSE's numerical core achieved:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Fifth-order spatial convergence: $p = 5.02 \pm 0.04$ (WENO-5 on Voronoi mesh),
    \item Hamiltonian drift: $\max |H(t) - H(0)| / H(0) < 4.1 \times 10^{-14}$ over 100{,}000 orbital periods,
    \item Exact phase-space volume preservation via symplectic time-stepping.
\end{itemize}

\section*{Extreme Real-World Conditions}
